ment about 90 percent  of the households in the Netherlands and  on the third slide on the third box on  your right hand you see the chart of the  fiber-to-the-home  networks in the Netherlands and what is  striking is that for those of you who  are not fully aware of the geography  geography of the Netherlands on the  right hand side is more relatively more  rural than the left hand side so  contrary to kind of an intuition in the  Netherlands you see that there is  actually more investment in fiber in  rural areas than in the urban areas and  what we aim with our decision or  wholesale access decision is to improve  and incentivize the parties to roll out  fiber in the light pink areas the more  urban areas in the Netherlands so  opening up access to the fixed network  of Vodafone ziggo ensures that kpn is no  longer the only nationwide network open  to third parties and this will create in  our opinion for the first time  infrastructure competition at  so level between kpn and Vodafone ziggo  and as a result it will lead to more  investments in our decision we've also  taken measures to make fiber investments  easier so where fiber is deployed by K  by K P n regulated access to the copper  network is withdrawn and this means that  there are no double network expenses for  KP n in addition we made access to  Vodafone Z goes Network conditional upon  Sirius access requests by third parties  only if there is a serious excess  request for wholesale services  Vodafone's ego has to open its network  but and because we wanted to avoid kind  of a theoretical a theoretical situation  but as far as we know now there is  serious interest to get access to  photophone Z goes Network which will  improve competition as a final point  what we did is that regulated access to  Vodafone's ago by KP n also a  possibility of course is excluded  because we want to stimulate and  incentivize KP n to compete at an  infrastructure level with Vodafone Z go  everywhere in the country further and  important and lastly an important aspect  of our decision is that we took a light  touch approach to price regulation in  principle ACM will only set prices for  excess when the parties cannot come to  an agreement we do hope that competition  will lead to competitive excess prices  and that we don't have to regulate  prices we are a regulator of lost resort  in our opinion but if it is necessary if  parties cannot come to an agreement we  will step in and set cost based access  prices but only if necessary so finally  wrapping up our wholesale fixed access  decision will lead in our opinion to  improve infrastructure competition  and in the long run it is infrastructure  competition that will push the network's  the boat to networks took and others to  continue to convert to invest in the  Netherlands this should lead to improve  networks and contribute to the best  broadband for everyone now and in the  future so for the next days ladies and  gentlemen while you are on your  conference journey remember that  whatever a session you attend in our  view what is all about is connectivity  and competition thank you very much  [Applause]
 

